WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.256
Browser-facing output deserves special
review because the browser interprets markup,

2
00:00:09.336 --> 00:00:12.838
scripts, URLs, styles, and events.

3
00:00:12.918 --> 00:00:20.390
React rendering provides useful escaping
behavior for normal text output,

4
00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:25.607
but that does not make every rendering
pattern safe.

5
00:00:25.687 --> 00:00:33.742
Dangerous HTML rendering, user-generated
content, Markdown transformations,

6
00:00:33.822 --> 00:00:42.228
rich text, URLs, attributes, and embedded
content all need context-aware handling.

7
00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:50.480
The question is whether untrusted content
can become active browser behavior in an

8
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.661
unintended context.

9
00:00:52.741 --> 00:01:00.563
CSRF is concerned with unwanted
state-changing requests made by a browser in an

10
00:01:00.643 --> 00:01:03.095
authenticated context.

11
00:01:03.175 --> 00:01:10.413
The exact controls depend on the
authentication design, request method,

12
00:01:10.493 --> 00:01:17.381
cookie behavior, and framework pattern,
but the principle is stable:

13
00:01:17.461 --> 00:01:22.248
browser credentials can be sent
automatically,

14
00:01:22.328 --> 00:01:30.383
so state-changing endpoints should have
protections appropriate to their risk.

15
00:01:30.463 --> 00:01:37.701
SameSite cookie behavior, explicit
anti-CSRF patterns, origin checks,

16
00:01:37.781 --> 00:01:45.603
and requiring deliberate user interaction
may all be relevant depending on the

17
00:01:45.683 --> 00:01:47.084
application.

18
00:01:47.164 --> 00:01:55.687
CORS controls which browser-based origins
are allowed to make cross-origin requests

19
00:01:55.767 --> 00:01:57.868
under browser rules.

20
00:01:57.948 --> 00:02:02.968
It is not authentication, it is not
authorization,

21
00:02:03.048 --> 00:02:07.484
and it does not protect non-browser
clients.

22
00:02:07.564 --> 00:02:15.036
Broad origins combined with credentials
can create unnecessary exposure.

23
00:02:15.116 --> 00:02:20.603
CORS policy should match the intended
browser clients,

24
00:02:20.683 --> 00:02:28.739
while server-side authentication and
authorization continue to enforce who may

25
00:02:28.819 --> 00:02:31.037
access or change data.

26
00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:37.771
Response safety also includes redirects,
response minimization,

27
00:02:37.851 --> 00:02:40.069
and security headers.

28
00:02:40.149 --> 00:02:47.504
Redirect targets should be constrained so
users are not sent to unintended

29
00:02:47.584 --> 00:02:49.102
destinations.

30
00:02:49.182 --> 00:02:56.537
Responses should return only the fields
needed for the caller and use case.

31
00:02:56.617 --> 00:03:03.038
Security headers, including Content
Security Policy concepts,

32
00:03:03.118 --> 00:03:09.189
can reduce browser-side risk when
configured thoughtfully.

33
00:03:09.269 --> 00:03:15.456
These controls work best when reviewed
together with routing,

34
00:03:15.536 --> 00:03:21.140
session behavior, output rendering, and
data exposure.
